# Organise household help with hub pages

**Reference pattern.** These examples are simulated. They do not install Home Assistant screens or control a device.

A hub gives people routes into groups of related help. Build it around what household members want to do, not around integrations and services (WG-001).

## Put the useful routes first

A hub has a few top shortcuts, then the main content groups, then less prominent related topics. Give each group a descriptive heading and a short line on what it holds. Keep top shortcuts few and base them on tasks people really do often. A top shortcut goes straight to its destination. It does not lead to the same middle page as a main card.

<%= example('nhs-hub-household') %>

## Keep cards in proportion

Use a hub for a substantial collection of help. For a few pages, a list is easier to scan and cheaper to keep up to date. Do not make a large card for every toggle, entity state or small setting.

<%= example('nhs-hub-small') %>

## Keep navigation apart from control

A room dashboard can have a route to room information and controls for lights. A navigation card has one destination and no button for a device action inside the same clickable area. A device card is a different component with its own labels, state and feedback. See [Cards](../components/cards.html#choose-the-card-by-its-job).

Do not put an unrelated image or icon on every help card. Use a domain icon only where it helps people recognise the group, and keep the text. Let quiet borders and spacing form the groups.

## Accessibility and content

Write heading links that make sense out of context (WG-083). Keep heading order and page order logical. The grid becomes one readable column at narrow width and high zoom. If a whole card is clickable, keep one real link, keep keyboard behaviour and avoid nested interactive elements. Keep focus visible with every theme colour.

## Home Assistant boundary

A hub links to help and settings pages that exist. It does not replace Home Assistant's navigation, hide native settings or guess routes for custom integrations. Respect profile permissions before showing an administrative link (WG-034). When a device is unavailable, keep its recovery help.

## Check the structure

Test the group names with household members. Check that they can find a task without knowing an integration name. For a consequential setup journey, use a [start page](start-page.html). For one complex topic, use a [mini-hub](mini-hub.html). A hub replaces neither. The preview links go to local explanations and never operate a device.
